course: dictionary
lesson: 1169
title: Words about home and bills
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Broadband is a fast internet connection. It lets a home, school or office send and receive lots of information at once. «There is rent or a mortgage, energy for heating and hot water, council tax, broadband and insurance.» The clues are the other words in the list: rent, energy, council tax and insurance are all things families pay for each month, so broadband must be a service you pay for too.
explain: Ask yourself what kind of word would fit in this list. It is not a place, a colour or a feeling. It is a thing you buy, so it fits with the bills. Then check your meaning by putting it in the sentence in place of the hard word: «There is rent, energy, council tax, a fast internet connection and insurance.» It still makes sense.
explain: Broadband can also describe a thing, as in «a broadband connection» or «superfast broadband». In computing lessons, pupils measure how fast a broadband connection is in megabits per second, shortened to Mbps.
fact: Broadband is a fast internet connection that can send a lot of information at once.
fact: Families and schools pay for broadband, usually every month.
fact: Speed is measured in megabits per second, shortened to Mbps.
fact: «Broadband» is an uncountable noun, so you say «some broadband», not «a broadband».
fact: Phrases you may hear: «the broadband is down», which means it has stopped working.
exercise: «There is rent or a mortgage, energy for heating and hot water, council tax, broadband and insurance.» What kind of thing is broadband in this sentence? | A wide road for cars | A room in the house | *A service a family pays for | A type of weather
exercise: «The broadband is down, so we cannot watch the film.» What does down mean here? | *It has stopped working | It is raining heavily | It has been sold | It is at the bottom of the house
exercise: In «a broadband connection», what kind of word is broadband? | A noun for a place | *An adjective, describing the connection | A verb for doing something | A number
test: «Fibre broadband is fast, so we can download films quickly.» What is the main clue that broadband means fast? | Fibre, which is a type of wire | Quickly, which means slowly | *Fast, and downloading films quickly | Films, which are on television
test: Which of these is the best way to measure how fast broadband is? | *Megabits per second | Litres per hour | Kilograms per metre | Hours per week
exam: In a bill list, «broadband» means a fast internet connection that families pay for, because the other words in the list are all bills, and you can check it by swapping in the meaning and seeing if the sentence still makes sense. | Not sure | *True | False
